    "evidence_text": "We found significant differences on the distribution of followers, the number of URLs on tweets, and the verification of the users.\n\nWe found significant differences on the distribution of followers, the number of URLs on tweets, and the verification of the users.\n\nthe associated distributions are equal.\n\nFigure FIGREF26 shows the distribution of favourites.\n\nDespite the apparent visual differences, the difference are not statistically significant.\n\nFigure FIGREF27 shows the density distribution of the number of hashtags used.\n\nHowever, the density distribution of friends from the accounts (Figure FIGREF29 ) shows that there is indeed a statistically significant difference in their distributions.\n\nHowever, the difference is not statistically significant.\n\nIn fact, the difference between the two distributions is statistically significant (assuming INLINEFORM0 ).\n\nWith most works so far focusing on the actual content of the tweets, ours is a novel attempt from a different, but also complementary, angle.\n\nWithin the used dataset, we found there are differences around exposure, characteristics of accounts spreading fake news and the tone of the content.\n\nWe plan to replicate and validate our experiments in an extended sample of tweets (until 4 months after the US election), and tests the predictive power of the features we found relevant within our sample.\n\nAuthor Disclosure Statement\tNo competing financial interest exist.",
